#{[{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046660} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186552592} :activity/id #uuid "b19fb14d-e45e-51a7-986d-db331034182c" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050104 :topic/id #uuid "2d9b53c7-d4e4-5c8d-ad01-c539428002fb" :topic/math-academy-id 1401} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186552580 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275232 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "02692810-69c5-51d1-8b75-972adfa093a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272403} {:answer/id #uuid "1c337dbd-c56c-53cc-8680-d3230277df72" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272632} {:answer/id #uuid "220fdb4d-991c-5f19-b039-9f36ce2bd775" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272689} {:answer/id #uuid "9c9b4fec-609c-5430-9e7e-dd4028a70321" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273744} {:answer/id #uuid "db2fc322-2cfa-560e-a0a2-d9894b64eb5e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274305}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272403} :answer-field/id #uuid "105e9ccb-1c80-52ca-9867-573d3e3315b0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274664}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "670ec23b-ca96-5b00-8ddc-e1d0726992ef" :question/math-academy-id "q-149685" :question/problem "The closed curve $C$ is defined as $r(t)=5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j$ for $t\\in [0,2\\pi ).$ The outward-pointing unit normal vector for $C$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\nn=\\frac{a(t)\\,i+b(t)\\,j}{||{r}^{′}(t)||}.\n$$\n\nFind the function $a(t).$\n\n*Hint: Observe that $C$ is traversed clockwise.*" :question/worked-solution "For convenience, we express $r(t)$ in column vector notation:\n\n$$\nr(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTo calculate the outward-pointing normal vector of a closed, *negatively* oriented curve, we follow three steps:\n\n**Step 1**: We compute the tangent vector ${r}^{′}(t).$\n\nDifferentiating the expression for $r(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\begin{bmatrix}{x}^{′}(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ -3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Since $C$ is oriented clockwise, we rotate ${r}^{′}(t)$ by ${90}^{∘}$ *counterclockwise* to obtain an outward-pointing normal vector.\n\nThe rotation matrix that corresponds to a ${90}^{∘}$ counterclockwise rotation is\n\n$$\nR=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nRotating the vector ${r}^{′}(t)$ by ${90}^{∘}$ counterclockwise, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ -3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Finally, we normalize the vector found in step 2.\n\nNote that the magnitude of the vector found in step 2 equals the magnitude of ${r}^{′}(t)$ (since they differ only by a rotation). Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =\\frac{1}{||{r}^{′}(t)||}\\begin{bmatrix}3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}=\\frac{3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j}{||{r}^{′}(t)||}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that $a(t)=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.$"} :step/id #uuid "913f14e8-4a70-5d0b-a3b9-b268ca739c0a"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186552582 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186445293 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "07f0b6be-b8a6-509c-9eef-fddd1e4c52cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445263} {:answer/id #uuid "4e0f7628-1647-5244-8346-f685935b3b5d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "36\\operatorname{sin}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445269} {:answer/id #uuid "b5c95c20-8a83-5b7f-a94b-48be5207dcd9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6\\operatorname{cos}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445278} {:answer/id #uuid "b5f1e04e-c989-540c-945e-5530f0a9ef5d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-6\\operatorname{cos}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445279} {:answer/id #uuid "ebba1cbb-de04-5f96-928a-2186ab923ede" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445285}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186445278} :answer-field/id #uuid "9f82b12c-6ce8-5ff6-b513-2e1efd6f0437" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445288}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "7df57c40-4642-538c-b446-11650ad2af53" :question/math-academy-id "q-126916" :question/problem "The closed curve $C$ is defined as $r(t)=6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+6\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j$ for $t\\in [0,2\\pi ).$ The outward-pointing unit normal vector for $C$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\nn=\\frac{a(t)\\,i+b(t)\\,j}{||{r}^{′}(t)||}.\n$$\n\nFind the function $b(t).$\n\n*Hint: Observe that $C$ is traversed clockwise.*" :question/worked-solution "For convenience, we express $r(t)$ in column vector notation:\n\n$$\nr(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 6\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTo calculate the outward-pointing normal vector of a closed, *negatively* oriented curve, we follow three steps:\n\n**Step 1**: We compute the tangent vector ${r}^{′}(t).$\n\nDifferentiating the expression for $r(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\begin{bmatrix}{x}^{′}(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}6\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ -6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Since $C$ is oriented clockwise, we rotate ${r}^{′}(t)$ by ${90}^{∘}$ *counterclockwise* to obtain an outward-pointing normal vector.\n\nThe rotation matrix that corresponds to a ${90}^{∘}$ counterclockwise rotation is\n\n$$\nR=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nRotating the vector ${r}^{′}(t)$ by ${90}^{∘}$ counterclockwise, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}6\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ -6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 6\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Finally, we normalize the vector found in step 2.\n\nNote that the magnitude of the vector found in step 2 equals the magnitude of ${r}^{′}(t)$ (since they differ only by a rotation). Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =\\frac{1}{||{r}^{′}(t)||}\\begin{bmatrix}6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 6\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}=\\frac{6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+6\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j}{||{r}^{′}(t)||}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that $b(t)=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.$"} :step/id #uuid "92f7ad67-f0dc-5b0d-8aa1-9e8dda63ee25" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186552611}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186552592 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106040 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0acdd021-7840-54cb-948b-ea5f4720bcd9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445264} {:answer/id #uuid "554fb461-f8fc-5f02-8c53-5d662d712545" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445270} {:answer/id #uuid "7c37312e-1d93-53a0-ba9c-7e273739a5c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498}