$\n\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nRotating the vector ${r}^{′}(t)$ by ${90}^{∘}$ clockwise, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ 3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Finally, we normalize the vector found in step 2.\n\nNote that the magnitude of the vector found in step 2 equals the magnitude of ${r}^{′}(t)$ (since they differ only by a rotation). Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =\\frac{1}{||{r}^{′}(t)||}\\begin{bmatrix}4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ 3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}=\\frac{4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j}{||{r}^{′}(t)||}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that $a(t)=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.$"} :step/id #uuid "aba2283e-e02e-5ef0-a6ef-347a3486ad65" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186552612}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186552611 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262673 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3fefda4d-70e4-50f7-a08f-145fda47c5e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-7\\operatorname{cos}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260435} {:answer/id #uuid "7440ed46-eeb3-5630-9a94-de0bbdfc268e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "7\\operatorname{sin}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260891} {:answer/id #uuid "930bf73e-0dec-5679-9db1-66acb37c6113" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "15\\operatorname{cos}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261154} {:answer/id #uuid "9d59d4b3-aa97-55cc-9383-959c5973b7e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "15\\operatorname{sin}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261236} {:answer/id #uuid "f471b9d0-7da6-5207-aaac-5ac655c24187" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "7\\operatorname{cos}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262009}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186261154} :answer-field/id #uuid "dfc55272-dded-5278-8d83-67249f477fae" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262514}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "ef0a8f8a-b901-5230-b4ab-2316cfe77836" :question/math-academy-id "q-126929" :question/problem "The closed curve $C$ is defined as $r(t)=15\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+7\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j$ for $t\\in [0,2\\pi ).$ The outward-pointing unit normal vector for $C$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\nn=\\frac{a(t)\\,i+b(t)\\,j}{||{r}^{′}(t)||}.\n$$\n\nFind the function $b(t).$\n\n*Hint: Observe that $C$ is traversed clockwise.*" :question/worked-solution "For convenience, we express $r(t)$ in column vector notation:\n\n$$\nr(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}15\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 7\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTo calculate the outward-pointing normal vector of a closed, *negatively* oriented curve, we follow three steps:\n\n**Step 1**: We compute the tangent vector ${r}^{′}(t).$\n\nDifferentiating the expression for $r(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\begin{bmatrix}{x}^{′}(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}15\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ -7\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Since $C$ is oriented clockwise, we rotate ${r}^{′}(t)$ by ${90}^{∘}$ *counterclockwise* to obtain an outward-pointing normal vector.\n\nThe rotation matrix that corresponds to a ${90}^{∘}$ counterclockwise rotation is\n\n$$\nR=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nRotating the vector ${r}^{′}(t)$ by ${90}^{∘}$ counterclockwise, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}15\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ -7\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}7\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 15\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Finally, we normalize the vector found in step 2.\n\nNote that the magnitude of the vector found in step 2 equals the magnitude of ${r}^{′}(t)$ (since they differ only by a rotation). Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =\\frac{1}{||{r}^{′}(t)||}\\begin{bmatrix}7\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 15\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}=\\frac{7\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+15\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j}{||{r}^{′}(t)||}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that $b(t)=15\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.$"} :step/id #uuid "de4f97fe-d7a2-566a-8438-2f341ab293fd" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186552580}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186552612 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106042 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "99f7b9ab-2895-5c57-9c75-8d6b8297740f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}3x \\\\ 3y\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102139} {:answer/id #uuid "a9636677-7c14-57f1-910d-66fb8733aea6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}\\begin{bmatrix}-x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102140} {:answer/id #uuid "dc408540-0c01-5568-aa45-f522fe602c71" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}\\begin{bmatrix}y \\\\ -x\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102141} {:answer/id #uuid "df16b44e-806d-5830-b5ec-407e2468a418" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102142} {:answer/id #uuid "77677902-7c56-5ae8-b1d9-1262045afd8d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102143}] :answer-field/id #uuid "b1eca71e-3deb-504b-9569-e86615218d4e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102138} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fca3eaa1-a46e-5f10-b465-7a2220b30d2c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}3x \\\\ 3y\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387728} {:answer/id #uuid "7b9735c9-78cb-54a4-a13e-f5ca8cff2938" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}\\begin{bmatrix}-x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387729} {:answer/id #uuid "d3afbba3-cfd4-5213-9e57-ab800cf1afbb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387730} {:answer/id #uuid "95b25916-d342-5451-85a5-0b2a1bdfd639" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}\\begin{bmatrix}y \\\\ -x\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387731} {:answer/id #uuid "0adf56d6-e77b-510f-b350-053cac7c2388" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387732}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387732} :answer-field/id #uuid "1751f1fe-03fe-5ebd-b2a0-3c1b8ca3cda5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387727}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "6ad4fbe5-37f0-5999-9ae5-70bbcca8a444" :question/math-academy-id "q-126830" :question/problem "The closed curve $C$ is defined as ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=9.$ Find the outward-pointing unit normal vector on $C.$" :question/worked-solution "We can parameterize $C$ as follows:\n\n$$\nr(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ 3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{bmatrix},\\,t\\in [0,2\\pi )\n$$\n\nTo calculate the outward-pointing normal vector of a closed, positively oriented curve, we follow three steps:\n\n**Step 1**: We compute the tangent vector ${r}^{′}(t).$\n\nDifferentiating the expression for $r(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\begin{bmatrix}{x}^{′}(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Since $C$ is oriented counterclockwise, we rotate ${r}^{′}(t)$ by ${90}^{∘}$ *clockwise* to obtain an outward-pointing normal vector.\n\nThe rotation matrix that corresponds to a ${90}^{∘}$ clockwise rotation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nRotating the vector ${r}^{′}(t)$ by ${90}^{∘}$ clockwise, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ 3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Finally, we normalize the vector found in step 2.\n\nNote that the magnitude of the vector found in step 2 equals the magnitude of ${r}^{′}(t)$ (since they differ only by a rotation).\n\nNow, since \n\n$$\n||{r}^{′}(t)||=\\sqrt{(-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}+(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}}=3,\n$$\n\nand since for our curve $x(t)=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t$ and $y(t)=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,$ it follows that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =\\frac{1}{||{r}^{′}(t)||}\\begin{bmatrix}3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ 3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\begin{bmatrix}3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ 3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "e1346a24-3003-59fc-9a8b-0c285dd57f7b" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186552582}}] :activity/title "Outward-Pointing Unit Normal Vectors in 2D" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551660}]}
